00:15:34,999 --> 00:15:38,779
And then once it's done
that, it's enabled cool down.

231
00:15:38,799 --> 00:15:42,909
That means we have an overheated
system that is running beyond

232
00:15:42,909 --> 00:15:44,299
CPU available, for example.

233
00:15:45,409 --> 00:15:47,449
We give it a certain
amount of time to resolve.

234
00:15:48,089 --> 00:15:52,189
We give it time to catch up with
processes that might have been

235
00:15:52,189 --> 00:15:57,529
slowed and latency was slowed, even
in a fully synchronous application.

236
00:15:57,529 --> 00:16:02,589
If it slows down, it might have to
wait for the application to cool down

237
00:16:02,759 --> 00:16:04,979
and calculate what it was supposed to.

238
00:16:06,019 --> 00:16:07,579
And finally, reconciliation.

239
00:16:07,599 --> 00:16:11,999
That means look again at the telemetry
and check that it works, and that

240
00:16:11,999 --> 00:16:13,729
in fact, the system has responded.

241
00:16:14,649 --> 00:16:20,629
In all cases, not merely being aware
of CPU, memory, GPU, but also impact.

242
00:16:21,819 --> 00:16:24,629
Is it making things slow down or crash?

243
00:16:25,629 --> 00:16:30,379
Once we've implemented this unified
logic, optimizations, instead of

244
00:16:30,379 --> 00:16:31,949
conflicting, can feed into each other.

245
00:16:33,029 --> 00:16:36,649
For example, more accurate pod
requests, whether in terms of the

246
00:16:36,669 --> 00:16:42,069
number of pods or their size, leads
to more optimal bin packing and

247
00:16:42,069 --> 00:16:44,659
then fewer underutilized nodes.

248
00:16:45,289 --> 00:16:49,879
Now the autoscaler can consolidate
and shut down unneeded resources.

249
00:16:51,439 --> 00:16:55,919
In conclusion, optimization
is your policy decision.

250
00:16:56,649 --> 00:16:59,889
How much latency is worth $100,000 to you?

251
00:16:59,889 --> 00:17:03,609
Today, these decisions are often
made intuitively by guessing,

252
00:17:03,609 --> 00:17:05,549
by panicking when things crash.

253
00:17:06,289 --> 00:17:11,129
Do you really know how much
downtime, how much latency will be

254
00:17:11,129 --> 00:17:12,829
caused by reducing your resources?

255
00:17:13,649 --> 00:17:16,669
Or conversely, how much it will
cost you to increase resources and

256
00:17:16,709 --> 00:17:21,079
allow some . This is a trade-off
that has to be monitored and

257
00:17:21,079 --> 00:17:22,689
calculated in an optimal way.

258
00:17:23,839 --> 00:17:31,369
Unifying the logic for different types of
metrics and scaling, and so stop scaling

259
00:17:31,439 --> 00:17:35,049
based on utilization of CPU or memory.

260
00:17:35,709 --> 00:17:42,089
Start scaling based on a holistic view
of cost, service health, and risk.

